// This is the config Semel builds itself with: the project's half (B-109).
//
// One file for the tree, beside the root Package.swift that semel.fmla names. Every node
// of the build -- the nested packages' readers and compilers included -- selects from
// the config file the root package owns; configuration does not inherit and nothing
// reads a config from anywhere else. SelfBuildConfigTests checks this file against what
// the tool types actually require, and that no nested package carries a copy.
//
// This tree is a Swift project only, so only the swift.* namespaces appear here. The
// machine's half -- the tool descriptors and the SDK -- is not here: `semel-swift prepare
// <checkout> --platform macos` writes it to semel.machine.config beside this file, one
// machine's facts, never committed. The same command vendors GRDB into Dependencies/ and
// keeps this file and semel.fmla as they are. Then, from the checkout's parent:
// `semel 'base .' 'build semel'`. The end-to-end roster does exactly that on a copy of
// the checkout (EndToEnd/Tests/Projects.swift, `semel`).
//
// There are no default values anywhere in Semel: every setting a node needs is written
// here once, under that node's namespace, or the build fails naming the missing key.
// moduleName and outputName are not here, on purpose: those come from the package
// manifest through node properties, and a config file must not be able to override
// what a target already is.
//
// The target is the platform at the deployment version the manifests declare, which is
// what `prepare` would write for a fresh tree.

swift.compiler.target=arm64-apple-macosx13.0

swift.linker.target=arm64-apple-macosx13.0
